Technological determinism encompasses a wide range of ideas used in practice, including technological impulses or technological imperatives, as well as a strict definition of the human destiny guided by hidden logic related to scientific laws and their manifestation in the field of technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism combine two general principles: firstly, the process of technological development itself is not influenced by cultural or political factors, and secondly, technology generates effects in societies that are immanent, rather than socially conditioned.
It can be said, for example, that a motor vehicle necessarily leads to the development of a road network.
Since the national road network is not economically viable when the number of vehicles is negligible, new production methods are developed in order to reduce vehicle operating costs.
Mass possession of cars also leads to an increase in the number of road accidents, and thus – to the invention in medicine of new ways of repairing damaged bodies.
Romanticism contained an important element of cultural determinism, which can be found in the writings of such authors as Goethe, Fichte and Schlegel.
In the context of romanticism, the individual was shaped by geography, and over time the customs and culture associated with this geography developed, so consistent with the place of society that they were better than arbitrarily imposed laws.
Just as Paris is famous today as the modern fashion capital, Constantinople was once recognized as the capital of fashion in feudal Europe.
His fame as a tabernacle of luxury had its beginning around the year 400 A.D
Its status decreased in the 12th century, primarily because the Crusaders returning from the expeditions brought such goods as silks and spices, which were valued more than what was offered at the Byzantine fair.
That's when the title of fashion capital was transferred from Constantinople to Paris.
