Scientific research conducted over the twentieth century shows that there are two pools of genetic variability – one hidden and the other expressed.
The mutation adds a new genetic variation, while selection removes the variation from the pool of expressed genes.
With each generation comes the mutual exchange of variability between the two pools through segregation and recombination.
Plants growing on the savannah with difficulty allow primates with the digestive system, such as in humans, to meet the body's demand for amino acids.
What's more, abandoning this can have serious consequences: halting growth, malnutrition, and finally death.
The most readily available plant resources would be the proteins contained in leaves and pods, but for primates such as us, they are difficult to digest without first cooking.
It is different with animal food (ants, termites, eggs), which is easily digested and provides large amounts of proteins containing all important amino acids.
Taking into account everything, it should come as no surprise that our ancestors solved their “protein problem” in a similar way, as chimpanzees do on the savannah today.
Sleeping interruption involves intentionally waking up during normal sleep and falling asleep again after 10–60 minutes.
