The domestication of goats probably took place in the Iranian Zagros mountains about 10,000 years ago.
People belonging to ancient cultures and tribes began to breed them because of their milk, bristles, meat and skins.
Domestic goats were usually kept in herds that wandered the hills or other pastures, often guarded by shepherd-like goat grovers, which were usually children or adolescents. These breeding methods are still used.
Railway tracks were built in England as early as the 16th century.
Although the tracks were built only in parallel wooden boards, train horses were able to move around them faster and transport larger loads than on then bumpy roads.
The railway sleepers on which the rails were attached appeared relatively early. Gradually, however, it was understood that in order to increase the efficiency of the tracks, it is necessary to mount iron strips on them.
This became a popular practice, but iron contributed to the greater consumption of wagon wheels made of wood.
At the end of the end, the wooden wheels were replaced with irons. In 1767, rails produced entirely from iron were introduced.
