When the declaration of independence was adopted by Congress on July 4, a handwritten version signed by John Hancock, President of Congress, and Secretary Charles Thomson was sent to John Dunlap's printing plant a few streets away.
During the night, 150 to 200 copies were printed, today known as “Dunlap broadsides.”
The documentary was first read publicly by John Nixon in the courtyard of the Independence Hall building on July 8.
One of them on July 6 was sent to George Washington, which instructed to read it to his troops on July 9 in New York. A copy went to London on August 10.
The 25 still-existing Dunlap tubers are the oldest copies of the document that have been preserved. The handy original did not survive.
