Generally speaking, when executives begin to lead their former teammates, there may be two types of behavior. One of them is trying to remain “one of the teams” (or one).
This kind of manager is reluctant to make unpopular decisions, avoid disciplinary actions and parties from evaluating performance, allocating tasks, and settling people out of their work.
The second extreme lies in unrecognizable transformation into someone who feels they need to change everything that the team has done so far, subduing it to their own vision.
In the end, the leader is ultimately responsible for the team’s successes and failures.
This behavior often results in frictions between leaders and other team members.
In the case of virtual teams, there are the same standards of excellence, but there are minor differences.
Members of the virtual team often function as a point of contact for a directly related physical group.
They often have more freedom than ordinary team members, because their teams can organize meetings according to different time zones, which local management may not understand.
The presence of a real “invisible team” (Larson and LaFasto, 1989, p.109) is a unique element of the virtual team.
“Invisible group” is the management team to which all members are subject. An invisible group sets standards for each member.
