The modern city of Casablanca was founded in the 10th century of BC by Berber fishermen and served the Phoenicians, the Romans and Merenidom as a strategic port called Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed them and rebuilt them under the name Casa Branca, but abandoned them after an earthquake of 1755.
The Moroccan sultan rebuilt the city as Daru-I-Badya, and the Spanish merchants, who had shopping bases there, called it Casablanca.
When it comes to shopping, Casablanca is in last place in all of Morocco.
Within the old Medina you can easily buy traditional Moroccan goods, e.g. tajins, ceramics, leather goods, water pipes and a whole lot of different trinkets, but it is an offer designed typically for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city in the Democratic Republic of Congo, located on the eastern end of the state close to the border with Rwanda.
In 2002, the city of Goma was destroyed when Nyiragongo volcano erupted and lava buried most of the city’s streets, especially its center.
While Goma is quite safe, all visits outside her area should have been investigated in advance for the state of the fighting taking place in North Kivu province.
From the city, you can go for a climb to the Nyiragongo volcano, as well as tracking down mountain gorillas, which is here one of the cheapest options of this attraction in Africa.
