Sundarbans are the world's largest mangrove forest belt, stretching 80 km (50 miles) from the coast deep into Bangladesh annd India.
Sundarbany has been included in the UNESCO World Heritage List. A part of the forest located on the territory of India is called the Sundarban National Park.
However, forests are not only mangroves – they are also the last remnants of huge jungles that once covered the Hindustan Lowlands.
Sundarbans have an area of 3850 km2, of which about a third are covered with waters and ureters.
