The Oyapock river bridge is a suspended bridge that enables communication between the city of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges de l'Oyapock in French Guiana.
The two towers have a height of 83 meters, its length is 378 meters and has two lanes with a width of 3.5 meters.
The vertical clearance of the bridge is 15 meters. The construction work was completed in August 2011, but the bridge was put into use only in March 2017.
According to the plan, the bridge will be able to be put into use in September 2017, when it is expected to complete the construction of Brazilian customs posts.
Guarani were the most important group of indigenous people in today's eastern Paraguay region, the life-leading half-nomadic hunters and hard-working agriculture to meet their own needs.
The Chaco region was the homeland of other indigenous tribes, such as Guaycurú and Payaguá, which survived thanks to hunting, harvesting and fishing.
Paraguay, formerly referred to as the "great province of India," was formed in the 16th century as a result of a clash between Spanish conquistadors and indigenous groups.
The Spaniards gave rise to a period of colonization that lasted three centuries.
Since the founding of Asunción in 1537, Paraguay has managed to retain much of its indigenous character and identity.
