The stirrups are the rider's foot supports that hang on each side of the saddle.
They provide the rider with greater stability, but they can cause safety concerns due to the possibility of the rider’s foot getting stuck in them.
If the rider is thrown off the horse and his foot is stuck in the stirrup, the fleeing horse can pull it. Various safety measures can be taken to minimize the risk of such an event.
First of all, most jockeys wear horseback riding shoes with heels and a smooth, quite narrow sole.
Subsequently, some saddles, in particular English, are equipped with safeguards, which cause the drains to fall away from the saddle if they are drawn by a rear-facing rider.
